Technical Note No. 3/2020/DBFLO summarized some of the conclusions reached by Ibama&#39;s technicians within the scope of this administrative process:
4.18. In accordance with Technical Note No. 2/2020/COMEX/CGMOC/DBFLO (SEI 6887773), which was signed by DBFLO and DIPRO staff, concerning issues were raised regarding the inefficiency in the application of IN No. 15/2011, especially concerning document analysis:
4.5. It is also important to point out that, although relevant for the environmental control of exported timber products, document analysis does not guarantee the regularity of the cargo or the chain of custody. The absence of a system capable of compiling data from the documents presented hinders control by Ibama, which can only &quot;see&quot; the printed documents presented by the exporter.
4.19. Furthermore, it was recognized that:
4.19.1. IN No. 13/2018 did not achieve its objective since the SisCITES module that revoked the authorization issued under Annex III of IN No. 15/2011 was not implemented;
4.19.2. IBAMA&#39;s decentralized units continued to issue the authorization provided for in Annex III of IN No. 15/2011, without explaining the reasons for doing so;
4.19.3. The presentation of such a document (IBAMA Authorization as per Annex III of IN No. 15/2011) by exporters is a requirement of many countries that import Brazilian wood;
4.19.4. Timber products and by-products that are not CITES listed or that do not fall under Article 5 of IN 15 do not have administrative treatment for Ibama. In other words, these products do not require LPCO (Licenses, Permits, Certificates and Others) in Siscomex, and can be exported without Ibama&#39;s approval;

